The current board has X pieces at bottom-row positions (5,2) and (5,3). This horizontal pair already threatens to become an open-ended three-in-a-row on the subsequent turn:
- X can play column 1, producing XXX on row 5 in columns 1-3 with both ends (columns 0 and 4) free;
- or X can play column 4, producing XXX on row 5 in columns 2-4 with both ends (columns 1 and 5) free.

In either case the resulting three-in-a-row possesses two simultaneous winning threats. Because a player can block only one column per turn, X would then win on the following move. Consequently any legal move other than column 1 or column 4 loses against perfect play.

Of the two saving moves, column 4 is strictly superior:
- it is closer to the center, preserving greater future mobility;
- it immediately forms a useful diagonal pair with the existing O stone at (4,3), creating a second long-term threat axis that column 1 does not create;
- the resulting position after O-(5,4) still leaves X without an immediate forced win, while the more peripheral stone O-(5,1) yields a weaker counter-play structure.

Thus the unique optimal action is to occupy column 4.

Final Answer: 4

